add("BG006","M103",2,"Q=[−R,R]^n is compact.",[[a+"2/text","R=max(L,0)+1 is a positive finite real, and Q is this coordinate order interval."],[d+"0/definition","Q is in the real tuple space."]],"Apply CompactIccSpace.isCompact_Icc using BG004–BG005. This proves the asserted box compactness without assuming it from its definition.");
add("BG007","M105",2,"For real a,b, int[a,b]=(a,b); in particular int J=(t0/2,3t0/2), and int[−R,R]=(−R,R).",[[a+"0/text","t0>0 and J has the stated real endpoints."],[a+"2/text","R>0 is established before using the box interior."]],"The real line has its dense order topology and has neither endpoints. These are exactly the probed interior_Icc hypotheses. In particular t0∈int J by ordered-field arithmetic.");
add("BG008","M104",2,"int Q is the coordinate box (−R,R)^n.",[[inp+"0/statement","The n-coordinate index set is finite."],[a+"2/text","Q is the product of the intervals [−R,R]."]],"interior_pi_set applies to the full finite coordinate index set; each coordinate interior is (−R,R) by BG007. Euclidean topology on a finite real tuple space is this product topology.");
add("BG009","M117",3,"Every open subset of V is measurable for the product of the real Lebesgue measurable structures.",[[d+"0/definition","dx is the product of n real Lebesgue measures and V has its usual tuple topology."],[inp+"0/statement","The n-coordinate index set is finite, hence countable."]],"Apply Pi.opensMeasurableSpace. Each real coordinate is second countable with measurable opens. Thus the product measurable structure used for dx has measurable opens; no identification of two different measures is being assumed.");
